     Since NetHack uses 8-bit characters for storing screen coordinates,
and some of them have to be able to hold negative values in some special
cases, trying to make the map wider than 127 is bound to cause problems.
Changing the typedefs for `uchar', `xchar', and `schar' to be short ints
instead of char might overcome this, but you'd probably be better off
limiting the map width to 120 or so.  Even if you get a "big maps" version
running, none of the object, monster, or room generation code will be
tuned sensibly.

